1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,799
We are Team VOID.

2
00:00:01,799 --> 00:00:07,610
This is VOID-NAV: a navigation system that keeps working when GPS goes dark.

3
00:00:07,610 --> 00:00:16,709
It senses the Earth's magnetic field, matches it against a map using two quantum algorithms, and gives a live position.

4
00:00:16,709 --> 00:00:20,914
And we can build it from scratch in 24-hours.

5
00:00:22,114 --> 00:00:23,655
Here is the problem.

6
00:00:23,655 --> 00:00:30,664
GPS signals are very weak, and they disappear in tunnels, bunkers, forests and under water.

7
00:00:30,664 --> 00:00:36,519
The usual backup is an inertial sensor, but its small errors keep adding up.

8
00:00:36,519 --> 00:00:41,448
A low-cost unit can be off by hundreds of metres within minutes.

9
00:00:41,448 --> 00:00:44,530
Good alternatives cost lakhs to crores.

10
00:00:45,730 --> 00:00:48,365
So our problem statement is simple.

11
00:00:48,365 --> 00:01:03,800
Give soldiers, vehicles and drones a position they can trust without GPS, using a sensor that emits nothing, costs under ₹5,000, and is ready for a quantum magnetometer when one is available.

12
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:06,661
This is use case UC-085.

13
00:01:07,861 --> 00:01:12,195
Our idea: every place has its own magnetic fingerprint.

14
00:01:12,195 --> 00:01:18,735
First, we walk the area once with GPS on and record the field in 2 m squares.

15
00:01:18,735 --> 00:01:20,705
That is our magnetic map.

16
00:01:20,705 --> 00:01:26,852
Then, with GPS off, the device records a short window of readings as it moves.

17
00:01:26,852 --> 00:01:35,126
Finally, quantum algorithms find where that window fits on the map, and the drifting track is corrected.

18
00:01:36,326 --> 00:01:38,260
This is the architecture.

19
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:44,373
An ESP32 reads the motion and magnetic sensors 50 times a second.

20
00:01:44,373 --> 00:01:57,216
A laptop or Raspberry Pi runs five steps: calibration, dead reckoning, Grover's search, the quantum kernel classifier, and a particle filter that combines everything.

21
00:01:57,216 --> 00:02:03,097
A dashboard shows the true path, the drifting path, and our corrected path.

22
00:02:04,296 --> 00:02:07,346
Quantum algorithm one is Grover's search.

23
00:02:07,346 --> 00:02:11,735
The map is split into 16 cells, stored in four qubits.

24
00:02:11,735 --> 00:02:16,794
The oracle marks the cells that match what the sensor just measured.

25
00:02:16,794 --> 00:02:24,233
The diffuser boosts those cells, so when we measure, the right cell comes out with high probability.

26
00:02:24,233 --> 00:02:26,762
A normal search checks every cell.

27
00:02:26,762 --> 00:02:29,515
Grover needs roughly the square root.

28
00:02:29,515 --> 00:02:35,466
At our small size this shows the principle; the benefit grows with larger maps.

29
00:02:36,666 --> 00:02:41,502
Quantum algorithm two is a quantum kernel support vector machine.

30
00:02:41,502 --> 00:02:48,571
Four features from each second of readings are encoded into four qubits with a ZZ feature map.

31
00:02:48,571 --> 00:02:53,705
The overlap between two encoded states tells us how similar they are.

32
00:02:53,705 --> 00:03:00,476
The classifier uses that to decide which zone we are in, such as a corridor or a stairwell.

33
00:03:00,476 --> 00:03:03,973
We compare it directly with a classical model.

34
00:03:05,173 --> 00:03:08,727
This table shows exactly where quantum is used.

35
00:03:08,727 --> 00:03:19,464
Grover's search and the quantum classifier are the two quantum algorithms, running on the Qiskit simulator, with one run on real IBM hardware.

36
00:03:19,464 --> 00:03:24,303
The sensor interface is ready for a future quantum magnetometer.

37
00:03:24,303 --> 00:03:27,403
Everything time-critical stays classical.

38
00:03:27,403 --> 00:03:31,562
That is how practical quantum systems are built today.

39
00:03:32,762 --> 00:03:42,249
This is the prototype: a sensor box on a small rover, with the magnetometer on a short mast to keep it away from motor noise.

40
00:03:42,249 --> 00:03:45,360
The same box can also be carried by hand.

41
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:50,294
The dashboard shows the live error for each method side by side.

42
00:03:51,494 --> 00:04:07,731
All the parts are available in India for about ₹4,500: an ESP32, a 9-axis sensor, a GPS module for mapping, a rover chassis with wheel encoders, a small display and a battery.

43
00:04:08,931 --> 00:04:11,299
Our tech stack is open source.

44
00:04:11,299 --> 00:04:13,352
Arduino code on the board.

45
00:04:13,352 --> 00:04:15,642
Qiskit for the quantum parts.

46
00:04:15,642 --> 00:04:17,379
Python for navigation.

47
00:04:17,379 --> 00:04:20,300
scikit-learn for classical baselines.

48
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:22,905
And Streamlit for the dashboard.

49
00:04:24,105 --> 00:04:31,204
Here is our 24-hour plan, split into hardware, quantum, and navigation work streams.

50
00:04:31,204 --> 00:04:36,241
By hour 12 we have the full pipeline working on recorded data.

51
00:04:36,241 --> 00:04:45,324
Every risk has a fallback: a handheld mode, a simulator if the quantum queue is long, and a recorded run for the demo.

52
00:04:46,524 --> 00:04:51,271
In the live demo, we show the map, switch GPS off and drive.

53
00:04:51,271 --> 00:04:53,644
The inertial path drifts away.

54
00:04:53,644 --> 00:05:01,002
Grover's search picks the right cell, the classifier names the zone, and our path snaps back.

55
00:05:01,002 --> 00:05:11,128
What is new: quantum search used for map matching, two quantum checks that must agree, and a design ready for a quantum sensor.

56
00:05:12,328 --> 00:05:22,722
Next: larger maps and outdoor trials within 6 months, and a diamond quantum magnetometer with defence partners within 18 months.

57
00:05:22,722 --> 00:05:29,372
Under ₹5,000, zero emissions, two quantum algorithms, 24-hours.

58
00:05:30,572 --> 00:05:35,223
When GPS goes dark, the ground still knows where you are.

59
00:05:35,223 --> 00:05:36,610
We are Team VOID.

60
00:05:36,610 --> 00:05:37,507
Thank you.
